- venue: bioRxiv
- citation count: 53
[method]
The paper used a Cre-driver-line-based projection mapping workflow: transgenic mice expressing Cre in defined cortical neuron populations were injected with Cre-dependent fluorescent tracers, and the resulting axonal terminals were mapped across the brain. The supplied section headings indicate that the paper characterized Cre driver lines for layer- and cell-class-selective mapping, used tracers and injection methods, acquired whole-brain images by serial two-photon tomography, processed the image data, registered the data to the Allen CCF, created top-down and flattened cortical views, and applied network modularity and clustering analyses. This relates to the proposed method because both rely on genetically defined cortical neuron classes, fluorescent tracer labeling, CCF-based anatomical quantification, and layer/class-specific projection mapping. The paper is not identical to the proposed plan: the supplied excerpt does not state that it used the six proposed Cre lines, SSp-bfd or SSs injections, iDISCO, MesoSPIM, or TrailMap, and it used serial two-photon tomography rather than the proposed light-sheet workflow.
[results]
The reported experimental outcome was the generation of layer- and cell-class-specific interareal cortical connectivity maps and the demonstration that intracortical connections are organized into modules. The paper also reports axon terminal lamination patterns related to source layer and cell class, anatomical rules for determining hierarchical position, an unsupervised hierarchy of cortical areas derived from layer termination patterns, and a hierarchy of cortical network modules. The supplied excerpt does not include numeric values, sample sizes, baseline comparisons, or statistical significance, so no quantitative outcome can be extracted from the provided text.
[context]
Key conditions included transgenic Cre-driver mice, fluorescent Cre-dependent tracers, cortical injection sites, serial two-photon tomography, CCF registration, and computational analyses including network modularity, clustering, and unsupervised hierarchy discovery. The section list also names Syt6-Cre_KI148, suggesting that at least one Cre line was characterized, but the provided text does not specify the exact number of animals, injection coordinates, viral titers, imaging voxel size, or statistical thresholds. Therefore, those parameters should not be assumed to match the proposed six-line SSp-bfd/SSs iDISCO/MesoSPIM/TrailMap experiment.
[end related work 1]

