- in_text_citation: [(Timonidis et al., 2023-10)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/f0ed01f29a2af2813b3ed77b87c3d7187e5e096d)
- venue: Neuroinformatics
- citation count: 2
[method]
The paper used a brain-wide projection-mapping pipeline for SSp-targeting experiments. Viral injections in SSp were used to label single neurons or micropopulations; whole-brain 3D images were registered to the Allen CCF, axonal projections were segmented, and projection maps were compared with population tracing data from the Allen Mouse Brain Connectivity Atlas. The pipeline also evaluated topographical precision by comparing focal micropopulation injections with proximal AMBCA experiments. This is directly analogous to the proposed method’s plan to inject Cre-dependent vectors into SSp-bfd and SSs, image cleared whole brains, segment axons with TrailMap, register to the CCF, and quantify projection density and topography.
[results]
The paper reported that single-neuron projections matched population tracing data for the SSp-targeting experiments, supporting the use of sparse single-neuron or micropopulation labeling to infer population-level projection patterns. It also reported that micropopulation experiments had higher topographical precision than proximal experiments from the AMBCA, indicating that smaller, more focal injections improve the spatial resolution of projection topography. The supplied excerpt does not provide numerical values, sample sizes, or statistical significance.
[context]
The relevant experiments were SSp-targeting brain-wide projection experiments, with single-neuron and micropopulation injection conditions, benchmarked against proximal AMBCA population tracing data. The analysis depended on whole-brain 3D imaging, CCF registration, axon segmentation, and topographical precision comparison. The supplied excerpt does not specify animal numbers, injection volumes, imaging resolution, segmentation parameters, or statistical tests, so those reproducibility details cannot be confirmed.
[end related work 2]

