- venue: Scientific Reports
- citation count: 30
[method]
The paper describes Intensify3D stack normalization, a computational image-processing approach for 3D microscopy stacks rather than a tissue-clearing protocol. It is applied to in vivo 2-photon and light-sheet microscopy data to address background complexity, correct penetration-related intensity changes, and restore distorted artificial 3D volumes. The method takes 3D image stacks as input and produces normalized stacks used for downstream neurite detection, soma detection, sphere quantification, and identification of anatomical structures. This is relevant to the proposed iDISCO/MesoSPIM workflow because the plan will acquire volumetric light-sheet images of cleared, immunolabeled brains and will need reliable intensity normalization before axon and soma quantification, but the paper does not describe iDISCO, MesoSPIM, TrailMap, or Allen CCF registration.
[results]
The supplied sections report qualitative outcomes rather than numerical metrics: correction of in vivo 2-photon imaging data facilitates accurate neurite detection and measurements; normalized light-sheet images enable precise identification of anatomical macro- and microstructures; auto-fluorescent light-sheet scans are used to extract accurate barrel field anatomy; correction of antibody and light penetration with Intensify3D facilitates accurate soma detection and quantification; distorted artificial 3D data are restored and detected spheres can be quantified; and the method is stated to be applicable to a large range of biological tissues. No specific quantitative values, baseline comparisons, or statistical significance tests are provided in the supplied sections.
[context]
The experimental context includes in vivo 2-photon imaging, light-sheet microscopy, auto-fluorescent light-sheet scans, artificial 3D data, and diverse biological tissues. The method addresses background complexity, antibody penetration, light penetration, and stack distortion. The supplied sections do not specify sample sizes, mouse lines, viral injections, iDISCO reagents, MesoSPIM hardware, 5 μm resolution, TrailMap training details, Allen CCF registration, or injection-site normalization. The barrel-field anatomy result is directly relevant to the research plan's focus on mouse whisker-related somatosensory cortices, while the soma and neurite quantification results are relevant to the plan's need to count labeled neurons and segment axons in volumetric cleared-tissue images.
[end related work 1]

