- venue: Molecular Brain
- citation count: 14
[method]
The paper used iDISCO+ immunolabeling and 3D imaging of mouse brains to visualize Fos expression after aversive visceral stimulation. Brains from mice exposed to LiCl or control conditions, with or without subdiaphragmatic vagotomy, were cleared and immunolabeled for Fos, then imaged in three dimensions to identify activated neurons, with emphasis on the insular cortex. This relates to the proposed iDISCO-based workflow because both approaches use solvent clearing and immunolabeling to prepare tissue for volumetric imaging, but the paper’s target was Fos-positive neuronal activation rather than Cre-dependent fluorescent axonal projections, and the provided excerpt does not describe MesoSPIM imaging, TrailMap segmentation, Allen CCF registration, or axon density normalization.
[results]
The reported outcomes were qualitative in the provided section headings: iDISCO+ analysis showed lateralized Fos activation in the insular cortex of adult female mice after aversive visceral stimulation; no obvious LiCl-induced insular activation was observed in young or aged male mice; intravenous LiCl produced no obvious Fos expression in the insular cortex; and subdiaphragmatic vagotomy affected Fos expression, although the direction and magnitude of this effect are not specified in the supplied excerpt. No numerical values, statistical tests, or baseline comparisons are available in the provided text.
[context]
The experimental context involved mouse models of aversive visceral stimuli, LiCl administration, intravenous injection controls, and subdiaphragmatic vagotomy, with comparisons across adult female, young male, and aged male mice. The method focused on the insular cortex as the region of interest. The supplied excerpt does not specify mouse strains, sample sizes, iDISCO+ reagent concentrations, imaging resolution, microscope type, registration framework, or statistical thresholds, so those reproducibility details cannot be reported.
