- in_text_citation: [(Ku et al., 2016-07)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/73ba61d149fbacecbf23c4d7b4587c76d1e25972)
- venue: Nature Biotechnology
- citation count: 445
[method]
The paper’s relevant method was a MAP expansion-microscopy workflow for 3D protein localization and neural-projection mapping. Tissues or cerebral organoids were perfused and embedded in a BSA hydrogel, then denatured and expanded to a size-adjustable scale. The expanded samples were subjected to shrinkage and refractive-index matching, immunostained with conventional antibodies, mounted, and imaged volumetrically. The workflow also included cell distortion analysis, tissue distortion analysis, and synaptic/fiber intensity profile analysis to assess structural fidelity and axonal/fiber signals. This relates to the proposed MesoSPIM method because both generate 3D volumes for quantifying axonal projections, but the paper’s approach is expansion-based super-resolution imaging rather than mesoscale light-sheet imaging at approximately 5 μm.
[results]
The supplied sections report qualitative outcomes: organ-scale tissue expansion without loss of proteins, preservation of multiscale architectures, successful labeling of the proteome with conventional antibodies, super-resolution imaging of 3D subcellular architectures, highly multiplexed immunolabeling, and MAPing of neural projections. No numerical expansion factors, resolution values, multiplexing counts, or statistical comparisons are available in the provided excerpt.
[context]
Key experimental conditions included BSA hydrogel embedding, size-adjustable expansion, shrinkage and RI matching, immunostaining of brain tissue, mounting and imaging, and distortion/fiber-intensity analyses. The specimens included cerebral organoids and brain tissue. The method is complementary to the research plan’s iDISCO/MesoSPIM/TrailMap/Allen CCF workflow: it could inform 3D axonal-projection quantification and validation of fiber morphology, but it does not replace the proposed 5 μm near-isotropic MesoSPIM acquisition for whole-brain mesoscale mapping.
[end related work 1]

