- in_text_citation: [(Vladimirov et al., 2023-12)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/04a0e991bdd8b3056d3409aa133514d3648cf100)
- venue: bioRxiv
- citation count: 8
[method]
The paper describes a low-cost, high-throughput mesoscale selective plane illumination microscopy (MesoSPIM) system and associated cleared-sample workflows for volumetric imaging of biological specimens. The microscope uses a 0.3 NA 5x objective, 405/488/561/640 nm excitation, an sCMOS camera, a CaF2 sample window, sample rotation with an ASI C60-3060-SRS rotary stage, focus interpolation, stitching/fusion, and an ASLM mode. Biological samples were prepared using iDISCO or vDISCO, including viral injection, tissue labeling, collection, and clearing, and then mounted for light-sheet imaging. This directly relates to the proposed research method, which will use MesoSPIM to acquire approximately 5 μm near-isotropic volumetric images of iDISCO-cleared mouse somatosensory cortex brains for axonal projection quantification. The paper does not itself describe TrailMap segmentation or Allen CCF registration, but it provides the imaging platform and cleared-sample workflow that would generate the 3D volumes used for those downstream analyses in the proposed plan.
[results]
The paper reports that the MesoSPIM system achieves near-isotropic resolution of approximately 5 μm and can image whole mouse brains and other cleared samples with usable contrast and field flatness across multiple wavelengths. It demonstrates high-throughput volumetric imaging, rotation/stitching, and 3D visualization of iDISCO mouse brains, vDISCO mouse brain/spinal cord/whole body, human brain, Xenopus, chicken embryo, and CaF2 crystal test objects. These outcomes support the feasibility of the proposed method’s requirement to generate 5 μm brain volumes suitable for TrailMap axon segmentation and Allen CCF registration. The reported outcomes are primarily technical feasibility and imaging-quality demonstrations rather than biological axonal projection quantification, and no statistical significance is reported for the imaging feasibility demonstrations.
[context]
Key experimental conditions include iDISCO or vDISCO clearing, fluorescent viral labeling, sample mounting in a CaF2 window, approximately 5 μm near-isotropic voxel size, 10 mm-scale field of view, multi-wavelength excitation, sample rotation, and a low-cost, transportable optomechanical design. The paper’s resolution measurement, objective contrast testing, chromatic field-flatness analysis, and high-throughput biological imaging provide the hardware and sample-preparation context for the proposed MesoSPIM acquisition of mouse brains. The relevant biological condition is iDISCO-cleared mouse brain with fluorescent viral labeling, which is analogous to the proposed Cre-dependent viral injection in transgenic mouse lines for mapping cell class-specific axonal projections.
[end related work 2]

