[method]
The paper describes a MesoSPIM-type mesoscale light-sheet microscopy workflow for acquiring volumetric images of cleared, labeled samples. The implementation includes an optomechanical design, sample rotation using an ASI C60-3060-SRS rotary stage, ASLM mode, focus interpolation, stitching/fusion, and 3D visualization. The paper also characterizes the microscope by testing objective contrast properties, measuring chromatic effects on field flatness, performing resolution measurements, and preparing CaF2 crystals as optical test samples. The biological workflow includes sample mounting, iDISCO mouse brain clearing, viral injection, tissue labeling/collection/clearing, vDISCO clearing of mouse brain/spinal cord/whole body, human brain tissue preparation, and Xenopus tropicalis sample preparation. This is directly related to the proposed method because the research plan will use MesoSPIM at approximately 5 μm near-isotropic resolution to image iDISCO-cleared mouse brains and generate 3D volumes for axonal projection quantification.
[results]
The paper reports successful high-throughput imaging of biological samples, including iDISCO/vDISCO-cleared mouse brain, human brain tissue, Xenopus samples, and CaF2 crystals, and reports resolution measurements consistent with approximately 5 μm near-isotropic mesoscale imaging. It also reports practical outcomes including cost reduction, travel capability, and upgrading of previous microscope versions.
[context]
Relevant experimental conditions are mesoscale light-sheet imaging of solvent-cleared, immunolabeled tissue; sample mounting compatible with rotation; use of an ASI C60-3060-SRS rotary stage; optical validation with CaF2 crystals and resolution measurements; and clearing/labeling protocols spanning iDISCO, vDISCO, human brain, and Xenopus preparations. The approximately 5 μm near-isotropic voxel size is the key condition linking the paper's MesoSPIM imaging to the proposed workflow, where the resulting volumes will be segmented with TrailMap and registered to the Allen CCF for cell-class-specific axonal projection analysis.
