- citation count: 9133
[method]
The paper used SNAP, a user-guided three-dimensional active contour and level-set segmentation tool for high-resolution human MRI. The workflow had three stages: selecting an evolution model, initializing the contour with spherical seeds or a prior mask, and setting weights for internal and external forces in either a casual verbal mode or a mathematical mode. Evolution was controlled interactively with play, stop, rewind, and single-step operations, with optional 3D region-of-interest restriction and resampling. The level-set result could be exported as a real-valued image or converted to a binary mask and merged with manual 3D masks. For validation, five MRI datasets were replicated three times and blinded to create 15 images. Three raters participated: rater A performed manual and SNAP segmentations, rater B performed only SNAP, and rater C performed only manual segmentation. Left and right caudate volumes and Dice overlap were computed for pairwise comparisons, and logit-transformed Dice values were analyzed with ANOVA including method, case, anatomy, repetition pair, and interaction terms. This method is related to the proposed TrailMap axon-voxel segmentation only as a 3D brain segmentation and validation framework; it does not segment axons, does not use a convolutional network, and targets caudate nuclei rather than axon-containing voxels.
[results]
SNAP caudate volumes were judged equivalent to manual expert volumes, and intraclass correlation analysis indicated excellent reliability for both SNAP and manual methods without a significant reliability difference between methods. SNAP-to-SNAP overlaps were significantly better than manual-manual or mixed-method overlaps; the worst SNAP pair still exceeded the best manual pair. ANOVA on logit-transformed Dice similarity coefficients found a significant method effect favoring SNAP (F = 1066, p < 0.001), a significant case effect (F = 5.176, p = 0.001), no significant effect of repetition pair or training (p = 0.287), and no significant left-versus-right caudate difference. A limitation was that the ANOVA included only rater A because only that rater used both methods.
[context]
The study used human morphologic MRI from a cohort of 56 autistic children, 11 developmentally delayed subjects, and 17 controls scanned at age two, with more than 100 images available. The validation subset contained five arbitrarily chosen MRI datasets replicated three times, yielding 15 images and 10 left/right caudate structures. Each structure had 12 segmentations from two raters, two methods, and three repetitions, producing 66 pairwise comparisons per structure. Metrics included caudate volumes, one-way random-effects intraclass correlation, Dice similarity coefficient, and logit-transformed Dice ANOVA. Specific active-contour weight values and computational hardware were not reported. The method involved sub-voxel level-set output that could lose precision when converted to a binary mask. This context is relevant to the proposed mouse iDISCO and MesoSPIM TrailMap workflow as a template for assessing 3D segmentation repeatability and spatial agreement, but the species, imaging modality, target structure, and algorithm differ.
