[method]
The paper used a brain-wide complete morphology pipeline in which sparsely labelled thalamic projection neurons were imaged after tissue clearing and mesoscale imaging, reconstructed as complete three-dimensional axonal morphologies, and registered to the Allen Mouse Brain Common Coordinate Framework. Registration was performed with mBrainAnalyzer: each imaged mouse brain was aligned to the Allen CCF template, and the reconstructed neuron coordinates and axonal arbors were transformed into CCF space. In CCF space, axonal arbors were partitioned into local and long-range components, assigned to CCFv3 brain regions, quantified for spatial distribution, and clustered to define projection neuron types. This is directly analogous to the proposed plan's step of registering TrailMap-segmented axonal density maps from SSp-bfd and SSs injections to the Allen CCF, because both approaches use CCF registration to convert sample-specific brain-wide projection data into a common anatomical space for region assignment, cross-sample comparison, and cross-class comparison.
[results]
The CCF-registered pipeline produced brain-wide complete axonal morphologies for a large thalamic projection neuron cohort, reported as 1,000 reconstructed neurons from 10 mice, and enabled population-level projection maps. Registration allowed the authors to assign axonal arbors to CCFv3 regions, compare individual single-neuron projections with mesoscale population-level projections, and identify distinct projection neuron categories, including cortical L2/3, L4, L5 IT, L5 ET, Car3, thalamic core/matrix, and striatal medium spiny neuron types. The paper also reported topographic organization of axonal projections, with single-neuron long-range projection patterns consistent with population-level maps. The supplied sections do not emphasize a formal registration-error benchmark or statistical test against a non-registered baseline; validation was based on overlay with CCF anatomy and agreement with known projection patterns.
[context]
The work was performed on mouse whole-brain mesoscale data using CCFv3 as the standard coordinate space. Key conditions included sparse fluorescent labelling of thalamic projection neurons, iDISCO-based clearing, MesoSPIM imaging at approximately 5 μm near-isotropic resolution, complete morphology reconstruction using Neuron-beta and mBrainAnalyzer-based processing, and CCF registration before arbor partitioning and quantification. The relevant experimental scale was a multi-mouse cohort of 10 mice and 1,000 reconstructed neurons, with CCFv3 region ontology used for anatomical assignment. For the proposed whisker-cortex plan, the analogous constraints would be registration of TrailMap axon-density volumes from iDISCO/MesoSPIM data to the same Allen CCF, followed by CCF region assignment and per-neuron normalization before comparing Rasgrf2, Scnn1a, Tlx3, Sim1, Rbp4, and Ntsr1 classes and SSp-bfd versus SSs injections. The paper's method is methodologically relevant but not directly equivalent to the proposed plan because it registers reconstructed single-neuron morphologies rather than TrailMap-segmented axon density maps and focuses on thalamic projection neurons rather than genetically defined whisker-cortex neuron classes.
[end related work 1]


