[method]
The paper used whole-brain imaging with real-time propidium iodide staining and mesoscale SPIM to reconstruct individual cortical projection neurons. After image preprocessing and registration to the Allen CCF, axonal signal was converted into 3D projection maps and assigned to categorical Allen CCF brain regions. Projection patterns were grouped into projection modes or classes, and the statistics included correlation-based comparisons of projection patterns and a correlation between projection complexity and dendritic complexity. This is related to the proposed method because it uses the same general strategy of quantifying projection similarity through categorical regional profiles and spatial comparison of 3D projection maps, although the paper used morphological or projection-mode classes rather than genetically defined Cre lines and did not compare SSp-bfd with SSs.
[results]
The analysis identified distinct, reproducible projection modes among the reconstructed cortical projection neurons, with approximately 1,000 neurons assigned to five major projection modes. The key quantitative outcome was a significant positive correlation between projection complexity and dendritic complexity, reported as roughly r = 0.4 with p < 0.001. The paper did not directly report a genetic cell-class comparison or an SSp-bfd versus SSs projection comparison, so its result is methodologically relevant rather than a direct test of the proposed hypothesis.
[context]
The study used mouse whole-brain volumes imaged at approximately 10 µm near-isotropic resolution using real-time PI staining and mesoscale SPIM. The atlas comprised about 1,000 reconstructed cortical projection neurons from a small set of mouse brains, registered to the Allen CCF for standardized regional assignment. Statistical comparisons used region-wise or voxel-wise correlation metrics and significance testing. The paper did not use iDISCO, TrailMap, Cre-dependent viral injections, normalization by labeled neuron number, or SSp-bfd/SSs-specific comparisons, so its relevance is to the proposed method's projection-map correlation framework rather than to the proposed genetic cell-class or whisker-cortex experimental design.
