  "support": "The strongest support comes from [(Peng et al., 2021-10)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/a867db7459aa116adc0401d85a92129e030713a4), which, as a newer extension of the [(Guo et al., 2017-06)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/48b1f98958a7a0d104b025fe0c81ac1ad276c25a) framework, used iDISCO immunolabeling/clearing, MesoSPIM at approximately 5 µm near-isotropic resolution, registration to the Allen CCF, and reconstruction of 1,000 complete neurons from 10 mouse brains to create categorical CCF-region projection profiles and compare 3D projection maps across neuron types. This directly validates the proposed use of categorical brain-region analysis and spatial comparison of 3D projection maps, and it shows that the proposed 5 µm iDISCO/MesoSPIM/CCF workflow is feasible for brain-wide projection quantification. This study also reported topographic organization in long-range projections and consistency between single-neuron and population-level maps for thalamic targets, supporting the proposed hypothesis that projection patterns can be organized and compared across defined populations, although their populations were not SSp-bfd or SSs. The next strongest support is [(Guo et al., 2017-06)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/48b1f98958a7a0d104b025fe0c81ac1ad276c25a), which reconstructed approximately 1,000 cortical projection neurons from mouse whole-brain volumes at approximately 10 µm resolution using real-time PI staining and mesoscale SPIM, registered them to the Allen CCF, assigned axonal signal to categorical brain regions, and used correlation-based comparisons to identify reproducible projection modes. This older study supports the general statistical framework of comparing projection patterns through regional profiles and 3D map correlation, and its significant positive correlation between projection complexity and dendritic complexity, roughly r = 0.4 with p < 0.001, shows that projection-pattern metrics can capture structured biological variation. The weakest but still relevant support is [(Fei et al., 2024-04)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/826415db924bd8ba1e98ed92f25778a23a0153ac), which used single-neuron projectome/tracing data, binary intrahemispheric connectivity matrices, computational network modeling, and wide-field calcium imaging with EEG and EMG to show that projection or connectivity patterns can be heterogeneous, asymmetric, and functionally meaningful. This newer work supports the broader question of whether spatial projection patterns are organized and functionally relevant, but it does not directly test genetically defined cell classes, iDISCO/MesoSPIM-derived 3D axonal maps, or direct spatial correlation of projection density maps, so its support for the proposed method is conceptual rather than methodological.",
  "contradictions": "The strongest caution is that [(Guo et al., 2017-06)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/48b1f98958a7a0d104b025fe0c81ac1ad276c25a) reported a significant positive correlation between projection complexity and dendritic complexity, roughly r = 0.4 with p < 0.001, in approximately 1,000 reconstructed cortical projection neurons. This quantitative result suggests that differences in 3D projection maps or categorical regional profiles may partly reflect overall neuronal morphological complexity rather than cell-class-specific long-range target selection. The proposed method normalizes axonal density by the number of labeled neurons in each injection site, but it does not explicitly control for dendritic complexity, soma size, local arbor volume, or viral expression intensity, so within-class and across-class projection correlations could be confounded by these morphological or labeling variables. The next strongest caution comes from [(Peng et al., 2021-10)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/a867db7459aa116adc0401d85a92129e030713a4), which showed that single-neuron thalamic projection maps were consistent with population-level patterns while preserving neuron-specific differences, and the provided excerpt did not report numerical correlation coefficients, effect sizes, or p-values for cross-class projection comparisons. This directly challenges the assumption that direct spatial correlation of individual 3D projection maps will be a stable primary metric; without population-level averaging, variance estimation, and rigorous statistical testing, pairwise spatial correlations may be dominated by individual variability, registration error, or sparse labeling. The weakest contradiction is from [(Fei et al., 2024-04)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/826415db924bd8ba1e98ed92f25778a23a0153ac), which reported highly heterogeneous and asymmetric connection patterns and characterized interhemispheric functional connectivity in terms of strength and stability, but the supplied excerpt lacked sample sizes, statistical tests, effect sizes, imaging resolution, registration framework, normalization, or baseline comparisons. This suggests that projection patterns may be too variable for simple categorical or spatial correlation to reveal strong class-specific structure, and that anatomical projection similarity may not automatically predict functional connectivity, but the evidence is limited by the absence of quantitative details.",
  "suggested_action": "Most importantly, the proposed method should adopt the [(Peng et al., 2021-10)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/a867db7459aa116adc0401d85a92129e030713a4) categorical CCF-region profile pipeline as the primary quantitative framework and report explicit statistics for within-class, across-class, and SSp-bfd versus SSs comparisons, including correlation coefficients, effect sizes, permutation or bootstrap confidence intervals, multiple-comparison correction, distance matrices, hierarchical clustering, and leave-one-out or cross-validation to distinguish class-specific structure from individual variability. Because the plan already normalizes axonal density by labeled neuron number, it should additionally normalize or regress out injection volume, viral titer, expression intensity, registration uncertainty, and TrailMap segmentation confidence, and it should compare both categorical regional profiles and direct 3D spatial correlation to show that the conclusions do not depend on one metric. Next, the method should address the [(Guo et al., 2017-06)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/48b1f98958a7a0d104b025fe0c81ac1ad276c25a) morphological confound by measuring or estimating dendritic complexity, soma size, or local arbor volume for labeled neurons, or by restricting the analysis to long-range axonal voxels outside the injection and local dendritic regions, and by including these variables as covariates when testing whether projection similarity remains significant across genetically defined cell classes. A further improvement is to follow the [(Peng et al., 2021-10)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/a867db7459aa116adc0401d85a92129e030713a4) observation that single-neuron maps differ from population maps by constructing population-level projection maps per Cre line and injection site, quantifying within-class variance, and using population-level maps for the main SSp-bfd versus SSs comparison while retaining single-neuron or single-sample maps for variability analyses. Finally, the functional validation should be strengthened by the [(Fei et al., 2024-04)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/826415db924bd8ba1e98ed92f25778a23a0153ac) emphasis on connectivity strength and stability: the optogenetic experiment should quantify the correlation between anatomical projection density and evoked calcium hotspots in motor cortex, report effect sizes and stability across stimulation locations, and test whether functional topography matches anatomical topography rather than merely showing evoked activity. These changes would make the proposed categorical and spatial correlation analysis more reproducible, less confounded, and better aligned with the strongest related evidence.",
